.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1252 PM PST Wed Nov 11 2026
(Friday through next Tuesday)

The first cold front is forecast to move into the North Bay by mid-
to-late Friday morning and then push southward across the Bay Area
and Central Coast in the afternoon and evening. Southerly winds will
increase ahead of the boundary and become out of the southwest just
ahead of the cold front. Overall, wind speeds have trended downward
with the latest set of guidance and as still forecast to be
strongest across the Pacific. There remains generally a 10%-15%
chance of thunderstorms across the North Bay and Bay Area on Friday
associated with the destabilization of the atmosphere. Rain totals
from this system are expected to be around 0.5-1.25" for the
North Bay (highest in the coastal ranges), 0.5-1.00" for the San
Francisco Bay Area, Monterey Bay region, and the Big Sur coast,
and 0.10-0.40" for the inland portions of Monterey and San Benito
Counties as well as the typical rain shadowed valleys.

On Saturday, mostly dry conditions are forecast over
the region aside from some post frontal rain showers into Friday
night into Saturday morning. However, temperatures will be
noticeably cooler with highs only reaching into the 60s across
most low-lying areas and upper 50s in the higher elevations (upper
40s in the peaks). Rainfall from this system is forecast to be
mostly beneficial. However, roadways will be slick and minor
nuisance flooding in the typical poorly drained areas. While the
wind speeds have trended lower, keep up with the latest forecast
information as things have the potential to change.

Another cold front is on tap to impact the region Sunday into Monday
with another one in the middle of the upcoming week. None of these
systems are forecast to stall over any one given region, reducing
the threat of widespread flooding concerns. Be sure to check back
for the latest forecast information as we head into an unsettled
weather pattern.

